<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  OWASP Dependency-Check suppression file for DigiBank (Workshop 2).

  Use this file to document and suppress specific findings that have been
  reviewed and accepted as false positives or otherwise not applicable,
  instead of silently ignoring the report. Each <suppress> entry must
  include a <notes> element explaining the reasoning.

  Schema: https://jeremylong.github.io/DependencyCheck/dependency-suppression.1.3.xsd
-->
<suppressions xmlns="https://jeremylong.github.io/DependencyCheck/dependency-suppression.1.3.xsd">
    <!--
    Workshop 2 note (2026-09-09): initial dependencies were reviewed manually against Maven
    Central metadata and bumped to the latest patch release within their existing minor line
    (spring-boot 3.3.2 -> 3.3.13, swagger-annotations 2.2.22 -> 2.2.29, cucumber 7.18.0 ->
    7.18.1, junit 5.10.2 -> 5.10.5). Once a full scan ran with a valid NVD_API_KEY, it flagged
    CVSS >= 7 findings on the 3.3.13 line (spring-boot/spring-core/log4j-api). Since 3.3.13
    is the last-ever 3.3.x release (EOL, no further patches), spring.boot.version was bumped
    to 3.5.16 (latest 3.5.x), which manages log4j2 2.24.3 and Spring Framework 6.2.19.
    -->

    <!--
    Workshop 2 note (2026-09-09): after the spring.boot.version bump to 3.5.16, a re-run of
    `mvn org.owasp:dependency-check-maven:check` still flags several CVEs on log4j-api-2.24.3
    and spring-core-6.2.19 with CVSS >= 7. Reviewed each finding individually below rather
    than blanket-suppressing the whole library, per the CVE description in the HTML report:

    - log4j-api (CVE-2026-34478/34479/34480/34481): all four describe a log injection /
      layout misconfiguration issue in Apache Log4j **Core**'s Rfc5424Layout (versions
      2.21.0-2.25.3). DigiBank never depends on log4j-core: log4j-api is pulled in only
      transitively as the log4j-to-slf4j bridge used by spring-boot-starter-logging, and the
      actual logging implementation is Logback. Dependency-Check's CPE matching conflates
      log4j-api with the generic "apache:log4j" product, causing a false positive here.

    - spring-core (CVE-2026-47890/47891/47892/47893/59282/59283/59313): every one of these
      explicitly targets Spring WebFlux (functional endpoints, Aalto XML parsing over
      WebFlux, WebFlux+DispatcherServlet CORS preflight, WebSocket exception headers),
      Server-Sent Events over view fragments, or SpEL expressions evaluated through
      SimpleEvaluationContext. DigiBank only depends on spring-boot-starter-web (classic
      annotated Spring MVC), has no WebFlux/reactor dependency, no SSE endpoints, and never
      evaluates user-supplied SpEL expressions (verified: no WebFlux/SimpleEvaluationContext/
      SseEmitter usage in the codebase). As of this review, Maven Central has not yet
      published a fixed Spring Framework release (6.2.20) that addresses these; suppressed
      here as not applicable to this codebase's architecture, to be revisited once a patched
      6.2.x/3.5.x release is available.
    -->
    <suppress>
        <notes><![CDATA[
        False positive: these CVEs describe a log injection issue in Apache Log4j Core's
        Rfc5424Layout (versions 2.21.0-2.25.3). DigiBank does not depend on log4j-core; this
        artifact is only the log4j-to-slf4j bridge pulled in by spring-boot-starter-logging,
        with Logback as the actual logging implementation. Reviewed 2026-09-09.
        ]]></notes>
        <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/org\.apache\.logging\.log4j/log4j-api@.*$</packageUrl>
        <cve>CVE-2026-34478</cve>
        <cve>CVE-2026-34479</cve>
        <cve>CVE-2026-34480</cve>
        <cve>CVE-2026-34481</cve>
    </suppress>

    <suppress>
        <notes><![CDATA[
        Not applicable: these CVEs target Spring WebFlux (functional endpoints, Aalto XML
        parsing, WebFlux+DispatcherServlet CORS preflight, WebSocket exception headers),
        Server-Sent Events over view fragments, or SpEL expressions evaluated through
        SimpleEvaluationContext. They apply to the whole Spring Framework 6.2.x release
        train (matched by Dependency-Check against every spring-* jar: spring-core,
        spring-aop, spring-beans, spring-context, spring-expression, spring-web,
        spring-webmvc, ...), not just spring-core. DigiBank only uses
        spring-boot-starter-web (annotated Spring MVC), has no WebFlux/reactor dependency,
        no SSE endpoints, and does not evaluate user-supplied SpEL expressions. No fixed
        Spring Framework release (6.2.20+) is published on Maven Central yet as of this
        review; revisit and remove this suppression once available. Reviewed 2026-09-09.
        ]]></notes>
        <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/org\.springframework/spring-.*@.*$</packageUrl>
        <cve>CVE-2026-47890</cve>
        <cve>CVE-2026-47891</cve>
        <cve>CVE-2026-47892</cve>
        <cve>CVE-2026-47893</cve>
        <cve>CVE-2026-59282</cve>
        <cve>CVE-2026-59283</cve>
        <cve>CVE-2026-59313</cve>
    </suppress>

    <suppress>
        <notes><![CDATA[
        False positive: CVE-2025-7962 describes an SMTP injection issue in "Jakarta Mail
        versions prior to 2.0.2". The artifact present here (angus-activation 2.0.3) is
        already newer than the stated fix version, and it is the MIME/activation framework
        artifact, not the angus-mail/Jakarta Mail messaging implementation the CVE actually
        targets (Dependency-Check's CPE match here has Low confidence: cpe:eclipse:angus_mail
        matched against angus-activation). DigiBank does not send email; this is a
        transitive dependency. Reviewed 2026-09-09.
        ]]></notes>
        <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/org\.eclipse\.angus/angus-activation@.*$</packageUrl>
        <cve>CVE-2025-7962</cve>
    </suppress>

    <!-- Example (kept commented as a template):
    <suppress>
        <notes><![CDATA[
        False positive: CVE-XXXX-XXXXX applies to a native module of this
        library that DigiBank does not use. Reviewed on 2026-09-09.
        ]]></notes>
        <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/some\.group/some-artifact@.*$</packageUrl>
        <cve>CVE-XXXX-XXXXX</cve>
    </suppress>
    -->
</suppressions>
